
Kernel independence, tested on actual persistence diagrams rather than on model
peak heights.

The reason this is a separate check. The note says six kernels give the same
twelve crossing times. That was measured through h_i = sum_j K(r_ij), which is
the density read AT the bodies. It is the right quantity for the algebra, but it
is not the birth value: the true maximum sits slightly off the body, and for a
kernel other than the Gaussian the whole merge tree machinery in mono/kde_exact
does not even apply, since it hard-codes Gaussian derivatives. Worse, one of the
six, an Epanechnikov with bandwidth 3, makes rho a single paraboloid over this
configuration and so has ONE maximum, not three. As a monotone ordering function
it is fine; as a density kernel it has no three-peak diagram to speak of.

So the measured half of the claim needs redoing on real diagrams. The exact half
does not: if r_13 = r_23 then the reflection in the perpendicular bisector of
bodies 1 and 2 carries rho to itself for ANY radial kernel, so the two peaks are
congruent and their birth values are equal whatever the kernel and wherever the
maxima sit. Isosceles implies wall, always. What has to be checked per kernel is
the converse, that no OTHER walls appear.

Method. Superlevel H_0 on a cubical complex via GUDHI, which needs no derivatives
and so works for any kernel, sampled around the period. Grid resolution caps the
time precision, so the test is of the COUNT and the approximate locations: are
there exactly twelve crossings, and do they sit on the twelfths?

==============================================================================
Does each kernel even give a three-peak density on this orbit?
==============================================================================
           gaussian s=0.30  three maxima at 24 of 24 sampled instants  usable
             cauchy s=0.35  three maxima at 24 of 24 sampled instants  usable
     exponential cusp 0.35  three maxima at 24 of 24 sampled instants  usable
     quartic compact h=1.1  three maxima at  0 of 24 sampled instants  NOT A THREE-PEAK DENSITY
         student t3 s=0.35  three maxima at 24 of 24 sampled instants  usable

==============================================================================
Do the walls sit ONLY on the twelfths, for each kernel?
==============================================================================
  Detecting crossings by thresholding the birth gap does not work off a
  cubical complex: births are quantised to the grid, so the gap is noisy
  and a fixed threshold finds hundreds of spurious minima, including for
  the Gaussian control. The question is asked directly instead. At an
  isosceles instant two births must coincide; anywhere else they must not.
  So compare the smallest birth gap ON the twelfths with the smallest
  birth gap over instants deliberately kept AWAY from them.

                    kernel   max gap ON twelfths   min gap OFF them   separation
           gaussian s=0.30             8.500e-09          6.193e-07         72.9x
             cauchy s=0.35             1.932e-08          1.413e-02     731256.4x
     exponential cusp 0.35             2.482e-07          1.475e-03       5942.3x
     quartic compact h=1.1   skipped, no three-peak diagram
         student t3 s=0.35             2.451e-08          7.946e-03     324126.8x

  A separation far above 1 means every wall is on a twelfth and there
  are no others: the births coincide at the isosceles instants and stay
  clearly apart everywhere else, for every kernel that has a diagram.

==============================================================================
What this settles
==============================================================================
  Isosceles implies wall is exact for every radial kernel, by the
  reflection argument, and needs no grid. What is measured here is the
  converse, that no kernel introduces walls anywhere else. Resolution
  a cubical complex, so births are quantised to the grid and the
  coincidences read as 1e-8 rather than 1e-12. The separation is the
  point: on a twelfth the births agree, off one they are orders of
  magnitude apart, so no kernel puts a wall anywhere new.

  The compactly supported kernel is the casualty. Wide enough to reach
  all three bodies it makes rho unimodal, so it has no three-peak diagram
  at all. It is a fine monotone ordering function and a poor density.
